Immigration detention
October 1, 2026
Immigration detention · October 1, 2026 · updated 2026-10-05
The Supreme Court agreed to decide whether people already in the country who were never admitted must be held without a bond hearing
The Court's October 1 order takes the case and decides nothing yet. Since July 2025 the government has said people arrested inside the country who had entered without inspection must be held, and the appeals courts have split.
The story
Just the facts
Show the work
In this story
What happened
The catch
Where this sits
What we do not know yet
The records
The grant
What happened
On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court agreed to decide whether the government must hold, while their removal cases are pending, people who are living in the United States and were never admitted. The order takes the case; it does not decide it. The detention statute deems a person present in the United States who has not been admitted an applicant for admission. The bond statute says the government "may release" a person on bond.
1
2
18
19
For about thirty years, by the Second Circuit's account, people who entered without inspection and were not admitted or paroled, and who were not apprehended while entering or shortly afterward or held on a criminal or terrorism ground, could ask for bond. On July 10, 2025, a memorandum from the commissioner of Customs and Border Protection said the detention statute covers them instead. On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals said immigration judges lack authority to grant them bond. On September 26, 2025, officers arrested Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts. An immigration judge denied him a bond hearing. A district court ordered a bond hearing or release within ten days. An immigration judge released him on bond after finding no danger and no flight risk. On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit ruled the other way from the Board in Barbosa da Cunha's case.
7
12
7
11
10
9
7
The detention statute uses two descriptions of the person, an applicant for admission and an alien seeking admission. It tells an immigration officer to detain that person for a removal proceeding when the officer decides the person seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.
18
Detention statute
·
2024 compilation
·
Inspection of applicants for admission
in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained
18
Government Publishing Office
Before 1996, the procedures for detaining and expelling a person depended largely on whether the person had physically entered the United States. Someone who entered illegally without being caught was eligible for bond. Someone who presented for inspection at a port of entry was to be detained unless clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to enter, or unless granted parole. In 1996 Congress enacted the removal and detention provisions this case concerns. The government's petition says Congress acted to correct that disparity. Congress replaced the defined term entry with admission: the lawful entry of a person into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer. Members of Congress described lawful admission, rather than physical entry, as the pivotal factor. The government contends that Barbosa da Cunha is subject to mandatory detention because he is an applicant for admission who is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted. It argues that "the ordinary meanings of the phrases 'applicant for admission' and 'seeking admission' are the same."
14
7
14
7
Barbosa da Cunha's response, dated August 21, says that in the detention provisions Congress largely kept the earlier scheme. On that account, people arrested inside the country, including "those who had entered without admission," remained eligible for release on bond, and people apprehended at the border continued to be detained without the possibility of bond. He says Congress kept that difference even as the law changed the removal process, combining deportation and exclusion into one removal procedure and making the grounds depend on admission rather than on physical entry. He says the law also deemed certain people applicants for admission, including people present in the United States who had not been admitted, which put people who entered without inspection on the same footing in removal proceedings as other applicants for admission. Even with those changes, he says, Congress kept the core of the earlier detention scheme, including release on bond for people who had entered without inspection and detention without bond for people seeking admission at the border.
6
The Second Circuit held that Barbosa da Cunha may be an applicant for admission but is not seeking admission, because he is not requesting lawful entry after inspection and authorization; he was already living here. So the court held that the detention statute does not apply to someone in the United States after entering without inspection and admission who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter. The Second Circuit affirmed the district court, which had required a bond hearing or release. Under the July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Parole is a separate power. The parole statute lets the government release a person "only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit". The July 10 memorandum says the only people eligible for release on bond are people already admitted.
7
10
7
10
U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit
·
April 28, 2026
·
Opinion
Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”
7
Barbosa da Cunha
Barbosa da Cunha entered around 2005, lived in the United States for more than twenty years, applied for asylum in 2016, and held a work permit. He has never been arrested for or charged with a crime. If the government wins, people in his situation cannot be released on bond under the detention statute. The Second Circuit wrote that if the detention statute applies, he "must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond". The question the Court agreed to decide is whether the detention statute requires that detention. Whether the Constitution separately requires a hearing is not part of the question. The Fourth Circuit names eight courts, the First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh, that held the bond statute applies to noncitizens inside the country, like the people who filed the cases, and that they must be given a bond hearing. On September 10, 2026, that court joined them. The Fifth and the Eighth held that the detention statute applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted, and that they must be detained without bond.
7
2
14
13
The government's petition asked the Court to wait on this case. It said the Court should hold this petition pending another petition, Lopez-Campos, which it called a "better vehicle" for resolving whether holding those people is lawful, and then dispose of this petition as appropriate. The October 1 order granted this petition.
14
1
The Court's order list for Thursday, October 1, 2026, grants the petitions for review in a group that includes Philip L. Rhoney, acting director, against Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha. The petition names Rhoney as Acting Field Office Director of the Buffalo Field Office of Immigration and Customs Enforcement, substituted for his predecessor.
1
14
record
The question the Court agreed to hear asks whether the detention statute requires holding, during removal proceedings, people who are in the United States and were not admitted.
2
record
The Court's list of cases set for argument is dated October 1, 2026. This case is on it with a grant date of October 1. That line does not set a day for argument.
3
record
The docket, opened July 23, 2026, records the petition that day, a response from Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha filed August 21, a reply from Philip L. Rhoney filed September 4, distribution for the September 28 conference, and a grant on October 1. On July 23 the same page said filings in this case should be submitted in paper form only, and should not be submitted through the Court's electronic filing system. The text of that response was posted on October 5. The docket lists that filing under August 21 and notes the PDF upload on October 5, and that entry includes a main document. That document is his response to the petition, dated August 21. The reply of September 4 is still listed, and the docket does not include its text. After the grant, the page says that if the Court schedules briefing and oral argument, the parties shall submit electronic versions of all prior and subsequent filings, and that subsequent filings should now be submitted through the electronic filing system.
5
6
record
Officers arrested him on September 26, 2025, while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts. A judge then ordered a bond hearing or release, and an immigration judge released him on bond.
7
record
The catch
CBS's tens of thousands
CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants during removal proceedings without bond hearings. The October 1 order, the Supreme Court's docket for this case, the Second Circuit's opinion, the Board of Immigration Appeals decision, and the July 10 Customs memorandum do not give a precise count of people held under the government's reading. The Fourth Circuit says district courts sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases, which counts court cases, not people in detention.
21
13
Who the policy reaches
Customs and Border Protection's public notice describes mandatory detention for applicants for admission the Department of Homeland Security encounters for the first time after they fail to lawfully present for inspection at a port of entry. The July 10 memorandum says the first encounter counts "regardless of when or where that first encounter occurs". So the policy reaches a person arrested inside the country years after entering: Barbosa da Cunha was arrested driving to work in Norwood more than twenty years after he entered.
11
10
7
The practice
Where this sits
The 1997 Federal Register rule made people present without having been admitted or paroled eligible for bond.
Date
Record
March 6, 1997
A Federal Register rule says people present without admission will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.
July 10, 2025
Customs and Border Protection says its commissioner issued detention guidance to every component.
Sept. 5, 2025
The Board of Immigration Appeals decides judges lack authority to grant bond in this situation.
Sept. 26, 2025
Officers arrested the man in this case while he was driving to work.
Apr. 28, 2026
The Second Circuit affirms the order requiring a bond hearing or release.
Sept. 10, 2026
The Fourth Circuit names the appeals courts that require a bond hearing and the two that do not.
Sept. 25, 2026
The Second Circuit denies rehearing by the full court.
Oct. 1, 2026
The Supreme Court grants review in this case.
On March 6, 1997, a Federal Register rule, effective April 1, said people present without having been admitted or paroled would be eligible for bond. On September 25, 2026, the full Second Circuit refused to rehear the case. Judge Steven J. Menashi dissented, arguing that the detention statute applies and that Barbosa da Cunha must be detained without a bond hearing. Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted against rehearing while writing that he agreed with the dissent's analysis. He wrote that mere substantive disagreement with the panel opinion does not warrant the time and effort of full court rehearing.
12
8
15
Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.
12
record
On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit affirmed the district court. It held that detention of a person in the United States after entering without inspection and admission, who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter, follows the bond statute, not mandatory detention. Judge Cabranes agreed and filed a separate opinion. The appeal was argued April 6.
7
record
In July 2025 the government changed its practice. The opinion cites immigration enforcement guidance dated July 8, 2025. The petition names that guidance as Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of Immigration and Customs Enforcement, and says applicants for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Divided panels in the Fifth and Eighth Circuits agreed with the government.
7
14
7
record
On September 25, 2026, the Second Circuit denied rehearing by the full court. Judges Bianco and Nathan, joined by four others, concurred in that denial. Judge Menashi dissented.
8
record
On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals held that immigration judges lack authority to hear bond requests, or to grant bond, for people present in the United States without admission.
9
record
The signed memorandum, a one-page scan, is addressed from Commissioner Rodney S. Scott. It says the department's position is that applicants for admission are subject to mandatory detention and may not be released except by parole.
10
record
Open questions
What we do not know yet
No argument date has been set. The Court took one question, what the detention statute means. Whether holding someone without a bond hearing violates the Constitution's guarantee of due process is not part of it; the government's petition says a second case, Lopez-Campos from the Sixth Circuit, raises that question as well. The Court had not granted or denied review in that case or in a third, Buenrostro-Mendez from the Fifth Circuit, as of October 3.
3
2
14
16
17
Customs and Border Protection's public notice says Commissioner Rodney S. Scott issued guidance on July 10, 2025, and that applicants for admission encountering the department for the first time after failing to lawfully present for inspection at a port of entry are subject to mandatory detention.
11
record
The question, the statutes, and one count in the coverage
The question presented reads: whether the cited detention provision mandates detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like the respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted.
2
The 2024 compilation of the detention section says that if the examining officer determines an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained for a removal proceeding. The bond section says the government may release an arrested alien on bond of at least $1,500.
18
19
70 days from the July 23 petition to the October 1 grant. 450 days from the July 8 guidance date in the opinion to the grant. 448 days from the July 10 Customs guidance to the grant. 391 days from the September 5 Board decision to the grant. Counted from those dates.
CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants without bond hearings. That count is not in the October 1 order, the Supreme Court's docket for this case, the Second Circuit's opinion, the Board of Immigration Appeals decision, or the July 10 Customs memorandum.
21
The Associated Press wrote that the Court agreed on Thursday to review a policy denying bond hearings. The Hill wrote that the Supreme Court announced Thursday it will decide the legality of President Trump's mandatory detention policy.
20
22
