data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt:50:9 requested a bond hearing, which the immigration judge denied on the grounds that respondent was subject to mandatory detention without the possibility of bond under Section 1225(b)(2)(A). See App., infra, 8a. 2. Since the government implemented its revised interpretation of Section 1225(b)(2)(A), thousands of aliens who are present in the country without having been admitted have filed petitions for habeas corpus seeking release from detention or a bond hearing under 8 U.S.C. 1226(a). Respondent is among that group. See App., infra, 70a. The day he was detained, he filed a habeas petition alleging that his detention without a bond hearing violated the INA and the Fifth Amendment’s Due Process Clause. Ibid. Although respondent initially filed his habeas petition in the United States District Court for the District of Massachusetts, his case was transferred to the United States District Court for the Western District of New York on the stipulation of the parties after respondent was moved to an immigration detention facility in Buffalo, New York. Ibid. The Western District of New York district court granted respondent’s habeas petition. App., infra, 69a85a. The court held that Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to respondent, and that instead his detention is governed by Section 1226(a), which permits release on bond or conditional parole while an alien’s removal proceedings unfold. Id. at 84a. In particular, the court determined that the phrase “ ‘seeking admission’ ” in Section 1225(b)(2)(A) “involves active efforts to obtain ‘admission,’ ” thus limiting the provision to aliens actively trying to obtain lawful entry after inspection by an immigration official. Id. at 79a. In the court’s view, this interpretation was necessary to avoid rendering language in Section 1225(b)(2)(A) superfluous. Id. at 80a.
