57-
58-        The government appeals from a grant of a writ of habeas corpus. Petitioner-
59-Appellee Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha (“Petitioner”) is a noncitizen from
60-Brazil, who entered the United States without inspection and admission in or
61-around 2005 and has lived in the United States ever since. He applied for asylum
62-in 2016 and was granted a valid work permit. In September 2025, the government
63:arrested him on an administrative warrant, placed him in removal proceedings,
64-and asserted that, while his removal proceedings are pending, he must be detained
65-pursuant to 8 U.S.C. § 1225(b)(2)(A). After an immigration judge found that he
66-was subject to mandatory detention under Section 1225(b)(2)(A), Petitioner
67-brought this habeas petition challenging his detention.
68-        The district court agreed with Petitioner that Section 1225(b)(2)(A) did not
69:apply to him and that, instead, his detention was governed by 8 U.S.C. § 1226(a),
70-under which he was eligible for release on bond. Thus, the district court ordered
71-the government to either provide him with a bond hearing or release him. An
72-immigration judge held such a hearing and, after determining that Petitioner did
73-not present a flight risk or danger to persons or property, ordered him released on
74-bond.
75-        On appeal, the government argues Petitioner is subject to mandatory
76-detention under Section 1225(b)(2)(A). We disagree. The plain text of both
77:Sections 1225(b)(2)(A) and 1226(a) make clear that only one applies to a noncitizen
78:like Petitioner:    Section 1226(a).    Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to
79-noncitizens, such as Petitioner, who are present in the United States after entering
80-the country without inspection and admission, and who were not apprehended at
81-
