96-challenged that settled practice despite making numerous amendments to the
97-immigration laws. Finally, even if the government’s newfound interpretation of
98-Section 1225(b)(2)(A) were plausible—and it is not—we would nonetheless reject
99-it based on our obligation to construe these statutes in a manner that would avoid
100-the serious constitutional questions attendant to what would be the broadest mass-
101-detention-without-bond mandate in our Nation’s history for millions of
102-noncitizens.
103-      Accordingly, we conclude that Petitioner’s detention is governed by Section
104:1226(a), not Section 1225(b)(2)(A), and we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the
105-writ of habeas corpus.
