167-      Petitioner, hoping to return to his family during the removal
168-proceedings, requested a bond hearing. For nearly thirty years, that
169-request would have been granted and Petitioner—whom the
170-government concedes poses no danger to the community nor risk of
171-flight—would have been released during the pendency of his removal
172-proceedings, pursuant to 8 U.S.C. § 1226. However, in July 2025, the
173-government changed that long-settled practice. See U.S. Immigr. &
174-Customs Enf’t, Interim Guidance Regarding Detention Authority for
175:Applicants for Admission (July 8, 2025).            To justify detaining
176-noncitizens (or “aliens” 1) who clearly meet the government’s own
177-criteria for release, the government began arguing that 8 U.S.C.
178-§ 1225(b)(2)(A) prohibits the setting of bond for all inadmissible
179-noncitizens, like Petitioner, unlawfully present in the United States—
180-
181-
182-1
183-  “The term ‘alien’ means any person not a citizen or national of the United
--
196-government claims that mandatory detention must continue
197-regardless of how long removal proceedings take—even if the
198-noncitizen poses no danger to the community or risk of flight.
199-      That is not what the law says. Today, although we part ways
200-with two other circuits that have addressed this question, we join the
201-overwhelming majority of federal judges across the Nation to
202-consider it and conclude that the government’s novel interpretation
203-of the immigration statutes defies their plain text. That text makes
204:clear that Section 1226(a) governs detention of noncitizens like
205-Petitioner. Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to such noncitizens,
206-who are present in the United States after entering the country
207-without inspection and admission, and who were not apprehended
208-while entering the country or shortly thereafter.
209-      This result is dictated by the plain text of these provisions, and
210-further confirmed by the statute’s context, structure, history, and
211-purpose. It likewise comports with the Supreme Court’s established
212-understanding of Sections 1225 and 1226. It reflects Executive Branch
--
233-mandate that the        government detain noncitizens who are
234-apprehended for removal proceedings while entering the country or
235-shortly thereafter, nor Section 1226(c)’s mandate that the government
236-detain noncitizens on certain criminal or terrorism-related grounds.
237-Instead, consistent with the plain text of the statutory provisions at
238-issue here, our holding allows noncitizens like Petitioner, who are
239-already present in the United States and are determined not to be a
240-flight risk or danger to the community, to be released on bond under
241:Section 1226(a) while their removal proceedings are pending.
242-      Accordingly, we conclude that Petitioner’s detention is
243:governed by Section 1226(a), not Section 1225(b)(2)(A), and we
244-AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus.
