## 1\. What You're Missing

**Major, new: the constitutional companion-case history is incomplete.** The current page says:

> “Whether holding someone without a bond hearing violates the Constitution's guarantee of due process is not part of it; the government's petition says a second case, Lopez-Campos from the Sixth Circuit, raises that question as well. The Court had not granted or denied review in that case or in a third, Buenrostro-Mendez from the Fifth Circuit, as of October 3.”

That leaves out a material part of **reader-model answer 6, what remains unresolved**. Before October 3, the Supreme Court had already dismissed a previously granted constitutional detention case, *Genalo v. G.M.*, and the government had filed a replacement petition, *Genalo v. D.C.*, No. 26-379.

The September 14 letter says: “On September 11, 2026, the Court dismissed Genalo v. G.M., No. 25-886, which was to be argued on October 13, 2026.” [Supreme Court](https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/25/25-1415/424170/20260914164801224_2026.09.14%20-%20Letter%20re%20Genalo%20Dismissal.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

The September 25 letter then says: “The Government has now filed a petition for certiorari that presents the same constitutional issues as those presented in G.M.” [Supreme Court](https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/25/25-1415/425716/20260925165614696_2026.09.25%20Letter%20re%20DC.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

The live [26-379 docket](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-379.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com) says: “Petition for a writ of certiorari before judgment filed. (Response due October 21, 2026).”

**Correct account:** Rhoney remains a statutory case. But the constitutional track did not consist only of Lopez-Campos. A previously granted constitutional vehicle was dismissed September 11, and the government filed a replacement constitutional petition September 21\. That petition remained pending in the final live check. No earlier audit in the supplied report set mentions Genalo, 25-886, or 26-379, so this finding is **new**.

## 2\. Is There More to the Story?

**Yes, materially.** The missing Genalo sequence changes the reader's understanding of what happened to the constitutional question alongside Rhoney. It shows that the Court had already agreed to hear a related prolonged-detention due-process dispute, lost that vehicle, and received a replacement before granting Rhoney on the statutory question.

Current coverage independently recognizes the same parallel track, describing 26-379 as the replacement for the dismissed Genalo case. [Latin Times](https://www.latintimes.com/pardon-killed-scotuss-top-immigration-case-heres-whats-stake-latinos-now-599710?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

## 3\. Completeness Verdict

**Incomplete.** The core Rhoney story is now strong: the page carries both sides' competing accounts of the 1996 change, the pre-2025 bond practice, the July 2025 reversal, Barbosa da Cunha's circumstances, the statutory hinge, the nationwide circuit split, the parole exception, and the limit of the October 1 grant.

The remaining Major omission is concentrated in the forward-looking constitutional context.

## 4\. Critical and Major Findings

There are **0 Critical findings and 1 Major finding**.

The Major finding is the new Genalo omission above. It changes reader-model answer 6 and the chronology of the constitutional litigation. The omitted development existed by September 25, before the page's stated October 3 status cutoff, so it is not merely an October 5 update.

## 5\. Before, After, and Around the Story

**Before:** The independent legislative-history check supports the page's current two-sided treatment. H.R. Rep. 104-469 says lawful admission would become the “pivotal factor” in status. H.R. Rep. 104-828 says new §236(a) “restates the current provisions” allowing the Attorney General to “release on bond an alien who is not lawfully in the United States.” Those records support why the parties can derive competing detention accounts from the 1996 legislation.

**Around:** The current page accurately carries the Second Circuit's reading, the government's reading, Barbosa da Cunha's response, and the Fourth Circuit's description of the split. The October 1 order indeed granted Rhoney. [Supreme Court](https://www.supremecourt.gov/orders/courtorders/100126zr_6j37.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

**After and parallel:** Rhoney still has no later merits disposition or argument date in the live docket. Lopez-Campos and Buenrostro-Mendez likewise showed no grant or denial in the final check. The missing parallel development is the Genalo dismissal and replacement petition.

## 6\. Accuracy and Verification

No material factual error was found in the current Rhoney account.

The live 26-104 docket still records the response as filed August 21 with its PDF uploaded October 5\. The September 4 government reply remains listed without a linked document. The October 1 entry remains “Petition GRANTED,” with no later merits ruling or set argument date found.

The Second Circuit record also shows a smaller implementation nuance: Barbosa da Cunha's September 2025 administrative arrest warrant cited §236 even though the immigration judge subsequently treated him as detained under §235\. That does not change a reader-model answer enough to constitute a story-view omission.

## 7\. Source and Corroboration Problems

The grant itself has a single controlling lineage, the Supreme Court's order and docket. AP, CBS, Hill, and later coverage are reporting on that underlying act rather than independent proof of it.

The manifest contains **no video pins or transcripts**, so there were no video records to inspect frame by frame.

The audit is **bounded** on the exact text of the new 26-379 petition. Its Supreme Court docket currently says filings should be submitted in paper form and provides no petition document link. The September 25 filing in the related cases is nevertheless a primary record stating that 26-379 presents the same constitutional issues as the dismissed Genalo case. [Supreme Court](https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/25/25-1415/425716/20260925165614696_2026.09.25%20Letter%20re%20DC.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

## 8\. Reconstructed Story

For decades after the 1996 overhaul, people arrested in the interior after entering without inspection were treated as eligible to seek bond. DHS changed that position in July 2025, the Board adopted the mandatory-detention interpretation, and Barbosa da Cunha was denied a hearing after his September arrest. A district court ordered a hearing or release, he obtained bond, and the Second Circuit held that an interior resident can be an “applicant for admission” without currently “seeking admission.” Other circuits split, and the Supreme Court granted Rhoney on October 1 to resolve that statutory question.

The constitutional question remains separate. The Court had granted a related prolonged-detention case, Genalo, but dismissed it September 11\. The government filed 26-379 as a replacement September 21\. Lopez-Campos also presents due-process issues connected to the current detention litigation. Rhoney itself does not put that constitutional question before the Court.

## 9\. Remaining Unknowns

The highest-value remaining check is the full 26-379 petition once obtainable, to state its questions and relationship to Rhoney without relying on the parties' September 25 characterization. The September 4 Rhoney reply also remains unavailable through the docket. A defensible population count for people detained specifically because of the July 2025 interpretation remains unestablished. No argument date for Rhoney was found in the final live check.

VERDICT: INCOMPLETE
