> “Whether holding someone without a bond hearing violates the Constitution's guarantee of due process is not part of it; the government's petition says a second case, Lopez-Campos from the Sixth Circuit, raises that question as well. The Court had not granted or denied review in that case or in a third, Buenrostro-Mendez from the Fifth Circuit, as of October 3.”

That leaves out a material part of **reader-model answer 6, what remains unresolved**. Before October 3, the Supreme Court had already dismissed a previously granted constitutional detention case, *Genalo v. G.M.*, and the government had filed a replacement petition, *Genalo v. D.C.*, No. 26-379.

The September 14 letter says: “On September 11, 2026, the Court dismissed Genalo v. G.M., No. 25-886, which was to be argued on October 13, 2026.” [Supreme Court](https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/25/25-1415/424170/20260914164801224_2026.09.14%20-%20Letter%20re%20Genalo%20Dismissal.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

The September 25 letter then says: “The Government has now filed a petition for certiorari that presents the same constitutional issues as those presented in G.M.” [Supreme Court](https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/25/25-1415/425716/20260925165614696_2026.09.25%20Letter%20re%20DC.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

The live [26-379 docket](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-379.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com) says: “Petition for a writ of certiorari before judgment filed. (Response due October 21, 2026).”

**Correct account:** Rhoney remains a statutory case. But the constitutional track did not consist only of Lopez-Campos. A previously granted constitutional vehicle was dismissed September 11, and the government filed a replacement constitutional petition September 21\. That petition remained pending in the final live check. No earlier audit in the supplied report set mentions Genalo, 25-886, or 26-379, so this finding is **new**.

## 2\. Is There More to the Story?

**Yes, materially.** The missing Genalo sequence changes the reader's understanding of what happened to the constitutional question alongside Rhoney. It shows that the Court had already agreed to hear a related prolonged-detention due-process dispute, lost that vehicle, and received a replacement before granting Rhoney on the statutory question.

Current coverage independently recognizes the same parallel track, describing 26-379 as the replacement for the dismissed Genalo case. [Latin Times](https://www.latintimes.com/pardon-killed-scotuss-top-immigration-case-heres-whats-stake-latinos-now-599710?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

